# AscendNPU-IR development guide

This rule governs work on the **AscendNPU-IR** project (aka BiShengIR):
an MLIR-based intermediate representation for compiling operators to Huawei
Ascend NPUs.

- Upstream (canonical): https://gitcode.com/Ascend/AscendNPU-IR
- Mirror (reference / browsing): https://github.com/Ascend/AscendNPU-IR
- License: Apache-2.0

When the working tree is the AscendNPU-IR source (it has `bishengir/`,
`build-tools/`, and `third-party/`), follow this guide. These companion skills
are available for routine actions: `pytest-runner`, `find-files`, `grep-search`,
`ssh-remote`, `scp-transfer` (see the other rules in `.cursor/rules/`).

## Repository layout

```
bishengir/                 Primary source
  include/bishengir/       Public headers (TableGen .td + C++ .h)
    Dialect/               One dir per dialect (see below)
    Conversion/ Transforms/ ...
  lib/                     Implementation (.cpp), mirrors include/ layout
  test/                    lit tests (*.mlir, RUN: lines) + test/Integration/
  tools/                   Binaries: bishengir-opt, bishengir-compile, etc.
  cmake/
build-tools/               build.sh and patches
third-party/               llvm-project, torch-mlir (git submodules)
docker/  docs/
CMakeLists.txt
```

### In-house dialects

The Ascend-specific dialects (under `Dialect/`) are **HFusion, HIVM, HACC,
Annotation, Scope** (plus HIVMAVE, HIVMRegbaseIntrins, AscendDPX and extensions
like MemRefExt / MathExt / TritonExt). The rest are upstream MLIR dialects
(Arith, Linalg, SCF, Tensor, Vector, MemRef, LLVMIR, Torch, Triton…).

When adding to a dialect, keep the `include/` and `lib/` trees in lockstep:
a dialect/op/pass has a `.td` (ODS) and/or `.h` under `include/bishengir/...`
and a matching `.cpp` under `lib/...`, plus a `CMakeLists.txt` entry.

## Environment

Source the CANN environment before building/running NPU-dependent pieces:

```bash
# CANN 8.5.0+
source ${PATH_TO_CANN}/cann/set_env.sh
# earlier
source ${PATH_TO_CANN}/ascend-toolkit/set_env.sh
```

Submodules are required:

```bash
git submodule update --init --recursive
```

## Build

Prefer the build script:

```bash
# Release
./build-tools/build.sh -o ./build --build-type Release
# Debug + tests, parallel
./build-tools/build.sh -o ./build --build-type Debug --build-test -j 64
# Reconfigure from scratch (fixes "ninja: error: loading 'build.ninja'")
./build-tools/build.sh -r -o ./build --build-type Debug
```

Common `build.sh` options: `-o/--build <path>`, `--build-type Release|Debug`,
`-r/--rebuild`, `-j/--jobs N`, `--install-prefix <path>`,
`--c-compiler`/`--cxx-compiler` (default clang/clang++), `--build-test`,
`--python-binding`, `--enable-cpu-runner`, `--enable-assertion`,
`--build-torch-mlir`, `--fast-build`, `--disable-ccache`,
`--add-cmake-options "<opts>"`.

Manual CMake+Ninja (equivalent):

```bash
mkdir -p build && cd build
export LLVM_SOURCE_DIR="$(realpath ../third-party/llvm-project)"
cmake ${LLVM_SOURCE_DIR}/llvm -G Ninja \
    -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=clang -DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=clang++ \
    -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
    -DLLVM_ENABLE_PROJECTS="mlir" \
    -DLLVM_EXTERNAL_PROJECTS="bishengir" \
    -DLLVM_EXTERNAL_BISHENGIR_SOURCE_DIR="$(realpath ..)" \
    -DBSPUB_DAVINCI_BISHENGIR=ON
ninja -j32
```

## Test

Always run the relevant tests after a change and before declaring done.

```bash
# lit regression tests (from the build dir)
cmake --build . --target "check-mlir;check-bishengir"
# or run lit directly on a dir or a single file
./bin/llvm-lit ../bishengir/test
./bin/llvm-lit ../bishengir/test/bishengir-compile/commandline.mlir
```

A run passes when the exit code is 0 and no tests failed; PASS, UNSUPPORTED,
and XFAIL are all acceptable outcomes.

For Python-side tests, use the pytest skill so output and errors are logged:

```bash
scripts/run_pytest.sh <file-or-dir> -l logs/<name>.log
```

## Writing a lit / FileCheck test

New compiler behavior needs a `*.mlir` test under `bishengir/test/` with a
`RUN:` line driving `bishengir-opt`/`bishengir-compile` and `FileCheck`:

```mlir
// RUN: bishengir-opt %s -your-pass | FileCheck %s
// CHECK-LABEL: func.func @example
func.func @example(...) -> ... {
  // CHECK: hivm.some_op
  ...
}
```

Mirror the conventions of neighboring tests in the same directory (check-prefix
usage, `-split-input-file`, `expected-error` for diagnostics).

## Coding conventions

- Follow **LLVM/MLIR style** (it is an LLVM external project): `clang-format`
  with the repo's `.clang-format`, LLVM naming (`CamelCase` types,
  `camelCase` functions/variables), no exceptions/RTTI, prefer `llvm::` and
  `mlir::` containers/utilities over STL where idiomatic.
- Ops are defined in **ODS/TableGen** (`.td`); regenerate via the build, never
  hand-edit generated `*.inc` files.
- Keep passes small and composable; register them in the matching
  `Passes.td`/`CMakeLists.txt`; document the pass with a summary/description.
- Match the **snake_case** doc-file naming used under `docs/`.
- Do not vendor changes into `third-party/` (llvm-project, torch-mlir) unless
  the task is explicitly about a submodule bump/patch; patches live in
  `build-tools/`.

## Agent workflow

1. Locate code with the `grep-search` / `find-files` skills before editing.
2. Make the change in both `include/` (.td/.h) and `lib/` (.cpp) as needed,
   plus `CMakeLists.txt`.
3. Build with `build.sh` (Debug + `--enable-assertion` while developing).
4. Add/adjust a lit test under `bishengir/test/`; run
   `check-bishengir` (and `check-mlir` if you touched shared infra).
5. Report the real build/test result (including the log path on failure); do
   not weaken or skip tests to get green.
6. Commit with a clear message; match existing commit style. Do not open a PR
   unless the user asks.
